<https://w3id.org/aias> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Ontology> .
<https://w3id.org/aias> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#versionIRI> <https://w3id.org/aias/2.0.0> .
<https://w3id.org/aias> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#imports> <https://w3id.org/aias/odp/iec60050/1.0.0> .
<https://w3id.org/aias> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#imports> <https://w3id.org/aias/odp/iso22989/2.0.0> .
<https://w3id.org/aias> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#imports> <https://w3id.org/aias/odp/iso7498/2.0.0> .
<https://w3id.org/aias> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#imports> <https://w3id.org/aias/odp/vdi3682/2.0.0> .
<https://w3id.org/aias> <http://purl.org/dc/terms/bibliographicCitation> "TODO: citation of the accompanying publication" .
<https://w3id.org/aias> <http://purl.org/dc/terms/contributor> "TODO: co-authors" .
<https://w3id.org/aias> <http://purl.org/dc/terms/created> "2026-08-24"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date> .
<https://w3id.org/aias> <http://purl.org/dc/terms/creator> "TODO: author name, ORCID" .
<https://w3id.org/aias> <http://purl.org/dc/terms/description> "The alignment ontology of the AIAS information model. It joins the four subdomain patterns into one model of an AI application in an automated plant.\n\nThe four patterns stand on their own and import nothing from one another. Each rests on a standard, and each is usable without the others. This ontology is the only place where classes of different namespaces are related, which keeps a pattern free of everything it does not need.\n\nIt is built around the system elements of Haberfellner: a function transforms, a component is transformed or performs a transformation, and a relation ties the two together. What each subdomain contributes hangs below one of those three, so that a process step of VDI 3682 and an inference of ISO/IEC 22989 become comparable without either standard having to know about the other.\n\nALIGNMENT.md records every bridge this ontology builds, the reason for it and the strength it is stated at."@en .
<https://w3id.org/aias> <http://purl.org/dc/terms/license> <https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/> .
<https://w3id.org/aias> <http://purl.org/dc/terms/publisher> "TODO: institution" .
<https://w3id.org/aias> <http://purl.org/dc/terms/rights> "TODO: funding statement, projects EKI and OptiFlex, dtec.bw" .
<https://w3id.org/aias> <http://purl.org/dc/terms/title> "AIAS Alignment Ontology"@en .
<https://w3id.org/aias> <http://purl.org/ontology/bibo/status> "Draft"@en .
<https://w3id.org/aias> <http://purl.org/vocab/vann/preferredNamespacePrefix> "aias" .
<https://w3id.org/aias> <http://purl.org/vocab/vann/preferredNamespaceUri> "https://w3id.org/aias#" .
<https://w3id.org/aias> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "Nothing here is normative. The patterns rest on standards, this ontology rests on design decisions of this work, and every class and relation says which of the two it is.\n\nTwo classes are related with owl:equivalentClass, and only where the definitions are word for word the same. Control is one of them: ISO/IEC 22989, 3.5.5 and IEC 60050-351, 351-42-19 carry the identical wording and trace back to the same source, which makes the two the same concept rather than similar ones. Everything else is a subclass relation or a relation of its own, because an equivalence claims more than an overlap supports.\n\nTwo traps are recorded rather than resolved. Controllability appears in two patterns with the same spelling and different meanings, one a property of a dynamic system and one a property of an AI system, and they are deliberately not related. The actuator of IEC 60050-351 is the signal side driving a valve, not the valve, so the device class of this ontology matches the final controlling element instead. ALIGNMENT.md gives both in full."@en .
<https://w3id.org/aias> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#versionInfo> "2.0.0" .
